h1("2 — The Constitution Record");
body("**Proposition 1 — The Constitution Record.**");
body("**Statement.** Every persisting configuration has a unique first record: the record that constitutes it.");
body("**Proof.** Axiom R orders records irreversibly (AP01, AP20): for any configuration, its records stand in a strict temporal order that cannot be unwritten. A persisting configuration has at least one record — persistence is exactly the holding-open of a record; a configuration with none has nothing held and is not a configuration. A non-empty, strictly ordered, irreversible set of records has a least element, and strictness makes it unique. That least element is the constitution record: the writing of the configuration into the arena. ∎");
body("The proposition is definition-level and carries no kill switch of its own. Its work is to name the object the rest of the paper prices: the snap is the writing of the constitution record, and everything that follows asks what that writing cost.");

// ============================== 3 ==============================
h1("3 — Held, Not Shed");
body("This is the load-bearing section. Everything numerical in §9 hangs on it, and its grade is stated exactly: derived at the structural register, with the record-algebra formalisation owed as D-CCC.1 and the claim guarded by KS-CCC.5.");
body("**Proposition 2 — The Held Commitment.**");
body("**Statement.** At a configuration’s constitution instant, the unpaired ε of that instant is held by the configuration as its persistence commitment. At every subsequent instant, the unpaired ε is shed — the leakage of AP06.");
body("**Proof.** Step 1. Every Actualization State instant produces exactly one unpaired ε that does not return to symmetry (AP43 D5, carried into Notebook IV by AP06 §6). This holds at the constitution instant as at every other: the instant’s inventory is the axiom’s inventory, 1:1 + 1×ε.");
body("Step 2. A configuration persists only if its constitution record is held open. This is Axiom R read as AP28 §2 reads it: “Without persistence, the crack heals, the symmetry restores, records cease.” Holding is not free-standing — something present must do it.");
body("Step 3. Enumerate what is present at the constitution instant. The axiom’s complete content at any instant is the 1:1 and the 1×ε — there is nothing else to enumerate. The 1:1 is not committable: AP10 §7 identifies the 1:1 as the substrate itself, the fifth degree of freedom, the probability dimension on which every configuration stands. Committing the stage to hold one record would not constitute a configuration; it would end the arena in which configurations occur. The only committable element present at the constitution instant is that instant’s 1×ε.");
body("Step 4. Suppose, for contradiction, that the constitution instant’s ε is shed. Then by Step 3 nothing committable remains at the instant to hold the constitution record; by Step 2 the record is not held; by Axiom R the crack heals and the configuration does not persist — contradicting the supposition that it is a persisting configuration. Therefore the constitution ε is held.");
body("Step 5. At every subsequent instant the constitution record already stands held: the commitment is in place, and the instant’s ε has no constitutive work to do. It is shed at the boundary. That shedding, instant after instant, is precisely the leakage AP06 measures — thermal, boundary-informed, C-bounded. ∎");
body("One mechanism, two destinations. The dichotomy the corpus needed — a once-cost beside a flow-cost — does not require two mechanisms. It requires one: an ε per instant, with the destination decided by whether the record it meets is being written or being held. The ε that meets a record being written becomes the commitment. Every ε after it becomes the leakage. The commitment is why the configuration exists; the leakage is what its existing costs the boundary from then on.");
body("The grade, exactly. The contradiction closes at the structural register: every step cites a locked result, and the exhaustiveness step (Step 3) rests on AP10 §7 rather than on assertion. What is owed is the formal expression — the held commitment as an operator on the record algebra, doing in symbols what Step 4 does in structure. That is D-CCC.1, and it is this paper’s standing debt in the sense that KS-30.1 is AP30’s: the argument is strong, named, and not yet a theorem.");

// ============================== 4 ==============================
h1("4 — One Snap, One ε");
body("**Proposition 3 — Per Configuration.**");
body("**Statement.** The commitment is exactly one ε per configuration, independent of the configuration’s channel count.");
body("**Proof.** Constitution is a single event: Proposition 1 gives each persisting configuration one constitution record, and the writing of one record is one Actualization State instant. One instant carries one unpaired ε (AP43 D5). A per-channel commitment would require per-channel constitution events — the configuration snapping channel by channel — and this is exactly what Axiom R forbids. AP28 §4, locked: “Axiom R does not permit selective persistence. The crack is open everywhere or nowhere.” A configuration constitutes entire or not at all. The same clause that forces the gravitational coupling to compound through all twenty-one channels at once forces the commitment to be paid once for all of them. One snap, one ε. ∎");
body("**Corollary — Ratio Invariance.** Every bound configuration is dressed by the same commitment factor, because the factor is per configuration and every configuration pays exactly one. In any ratio of two masses, the factor divides out identically — not approximately, identically. The proton-to-electron mass ratio of AP30 therefore carries no commitment signature at any order: the agreement inside experimental uncertainty is preserved to the digit, and AP30 §7’s reading of its own residual — “It is not a missing term” — stands exactly as locked. ∎");
body("The corollary is guarded by KS-CCC.2, and it is worth saying why the guard is unforgiving. A channel-scaled cost — the proton paying twenty-one shares to the electron’s one — would shift the mass ratio at eight orders of magnitude above what measurement allows. The per-configuration count is not a modelling preference. It is the only count that survives contact with the corpus’s own strongest number, and it is the count the axiom forces anyway. When the forced answer and the surviving answer are the same answer, the structure is telling you it is complete.");

// ============================== 5 ==============================
h1("5 — Realised over Provisioned");
body("**Proposition 4 — The Form.**");
body("**Statement.** The realised coupling is the structural coupling divided by (1 + ε): α_G* = α_G / (1 + ε), with ε = α_em by Identification 6.1.");
body("**Proof.** The axiom names both totals. What the snap provisions is its full right-hand side: 1:1 + 1×ε — the balance, plus the one element whose holding makes the balance persist (Proposition 2). What persists as the realised configuration is the 1:1. The realised fraction of the provisioned is therefore 1 / (1 + ε): the balance over the balance-plus-commitment. The correction is a quotient because the axiom is a sum. ∎");
body("The first-order shadow (1 − ε) differs from the quotient at order ε² — about 53 parts per million in G — below anything current metrology can resolve. The structural form is the quotient, because the quotient is what the axiom writes, and the paper states the quotient. The value of ε in the quotient is α_em: the commitment is read at the coupling boundary, and the coupling boundary’s reading of ε is Identification 6.1’s, inherited from AP06 exactly as AP28 inherits it. This paper stands and falls with AP28 on that shared floor and adds no identification of its own.");

// ============================== 6 ==============================
h1("6 — The Placement");
body("**Proposition 5 — Coupling-Side Placement.**");
body("**Statement.** The commitment divides the persistence coupling. It does not dress the masses.");
body("**Argument.** A held commitment has two candidate structural homes: the coupling that does the holding, or the masses being held. The mass-side home fails twice. First, the arithmetic: G carries the electron mass squared in its denominator, so a mass dressing must enter as (1 + ε/2) per mass to reproduce a single factor of (1 + ε) in G — and the half is forced by nothing in the axiom. A structural cost with an unforced coefficient is not a structural cost. Second, the double count: AP29 establishes E ≡ κ(B) · AS — mass is coupling capacity, held and actualised. The commitment is held coupling capacity. To dress the mass with the commitment is to book the same held capacity twice, once as the mass and once as the cost of the mass. The coupling-side home has neither defect: the commitment enters once, as the committed fraction of the coupling itself. α_G* = α_G / (1 + ε); κ* = α_G* ℏc / 2. ∎");
body("The grade is stated in AP28’s own currency: this is a selection among structural candidates — the epistemic class of the 1/π normalisation — with the elimination argued and the exhaustiveness of the two-candidate enumeration owed as D-CCC.4.");
body("The placement forces the paper’s entire footprint, and the footprint is the proposal’s discipline. Mass ratios: untouched (§4). The acceleration floor a₀ = C_S² · cH₀ / (2π), the Hubble prediction, and the dark-sector fractions: untouched — none contains α_G. The nuclear binding ceiling of AP30 §8: untouched — it carries the puncture factor, not the compounded coupling. What moves: G, and the holding limit κ, whose dependents (AP14, The Lock) are to be audited before adoption (D-CCC.3). A structural cost that touched everything would explain nothing. This one touches the two quantities that hold, and only them — which is what a persistence price should do.");

// ============================== 7 ==============================
h1("7 — The Double Entry");
body("**Proposition 6 — Two Readings, One Element.**");
body("**Statement.** The puncture factor (1 + 1/π) and the commitment quotient 1/(1 + ε) are two independent readings of the one unpaired element — what it contributes and what it committed — and their joint appearance in the realised formula is not double-counting.");
body("**Argument.** The licence is AP24’s, and it was locked before this paper existed: one ε, many independent readings, each through a different instrument, none double-counting another. The corpus already reads the same ε as geometry in G and as propagation in c without apology, because a projection is not a portion. Apply the licence here. The puncture factor is the geometric reading of the unpaired element (AP28, Proposition 4): the capacity its round hole adds to the coupling — what the element contributes. The commitment is the persistence reading of the same element (§3): the provision its constitution required — what the element cost. Contribution and cost are different questions asked of one thing, and a ledger that records both is not counting one entry twice; it is keeping both columns. The realised formula carries the axiom in full: the twenty-one paired channels are the 1:1; the puncture factor and the commitment are the 1×ε, read out and read in. ∎");
body("The guard is KS-CCC.6, and it is coupled to a debt the corpus already carries. AP28’s KS-R.9 sub-debt owes the formal normalisation of the boundary coupling. If that calculation, when completed, is shown to already contain the persistence provision — one reading, not two — then the commitment double-counts, the correction is removed, and the puncture factor absorbs it under a reinterpreted normalisation. The switch is stated so that the hostile reading of this section — “first a factor was added to fit, now a factor is divided to fit better” — meets its answer in the register where it belongs: a named test, not a rhetorical defence.");

// ============================== 8 ==============================
h1("8 — The Vertices");
body("The count is already proven; only the reading is new. AP28 Propositions 1–2 establish the coupling structure of the arena: six faces of ε, three spatial dimensions, one Actualization State. Faces couple to dimensions — eighteen edges. The Actualization State couples to dimensions — three edges. Faces do not couple to the Actualization State — Proposition 2, the page and the pen. Twenty-one edges. And the same proven structure has 6 + 3 + 1 = **ten vertices**.");
body("The two costs of §1 are the two counts of this one graph. Leakage is transmission — an edge phenomenon: it flows along the couplings, per instant, C-bounded, twenty-one wide. Constitution is existence — a vertex phenomenon: the configuration is written into the elements themselves, once, R-recorded, ten deep. The corpus’s ten stands to its twenty-one as node to edge, as constitution to flow, as once to always. Nothing was added to obtain the ten. It was standing in AP28’s proof the whole time, waiting for the question that counts vertices instead of edges.");
body("Honesty about status, in two halves. The count is DERIVED — it is the vertex set of a proven graph. The assignment — the commitment distributed one share per vertex, compounding to the quotient of §5 — is MOTIVATED, not forced, and it is booked as such (D-CCC.2), the exact mirror of KS-30.4’s uniqueness debt in AP30. And the assignment is empirically silent: the distributed and undistributed forms differ by about 24 parts per million in G, below anything the laboratories can currently see. Because §4 fixes the total at one ε regardless of any distribution, nothing numerical in this paper rests on the vertices. They are architecture — the right architecture, this paper holds — and architecture is claimed as architecture, not smuggled in as arithmetic.");

// ============================== 9 ==============================
h1("9 — The Prediction");
body("The realised gravitational coupling is α_G* = α_em²¹ × (1 + 1/π) / (1 + α_em), and the realised constant is:");
body("**G = α_em²¹ × (1 + 1/π) / (1 + α_em) × ℏc / m_e²**", { align: AlignmentType.CENTER });
body("Using the CODATA inputs, as AP28 uses them:");
body("α_em²¹ = 1.338 × 10⁻⁴⁵", { align: AlignmentType.CENTER, after: 40 });
body("1 + 1/π = 1.31831", { align: AlignmentType.CENTER, after: 40 });
body("1 / (1 + α_em) = 0.992756", { align: AlignmentType.CENTER, after: 40 });
body("Structural value (AP28): G = 6.7206 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²", { align: AlignmentType.CENTER, after: 40 });
body("**Realised value: G = 6.6719 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²**", { align: AlignmentType.CENTER, after: 40 });
body("Measured (CODATA 2018, unchanged 2022): G = 6.6743 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²", { align: AlignmentType.CENTER });
body("**Residual: −0.036%.**");
body("Read against the landscape rather than against a single number. AP28’s structural value stands 0.69% above the recommended value — thirteen times the roughly 500 ppm spread between independent laboratory determinations, outside every experiment ever performed. The realised value stands about 360 ppm below the recommended value — inside that spread, within one standard uncertainty of the lowest published determination and below others. The commitment closes about ninety-five per cent of the discrepancy and moves the prediction from outside the experimental landscape to its low edge. That is what the correction does. What it does not do is land on the recommended central value, and the paper says so in the same breath.");
body("The sign flip, stated plainly. Under AP28 alone, the gap ran prediction-above-measurement, the direction downward apparatus leakage can explain — Ø Predictions Part II says exactly this. Under the commitment, the residual runs prediction-below-measurement, and the same leakage mechanism cannot absorb it: leakage lowers what laboratories read; it cannot raise it. Adopting this paper therefore trades a large residual with a comfortable story for a small residual with no story yet. The residual is reported open and unattributed (§0.7): laboratory convergence toward the low determinations, a higher-order term in ε, or a refinement not yet forced. Which of these, the data will say — under the switches of §12 — and this paper will not say first.");

// ============================== 10 ==============================
h1("10 — What the Data May and May Not Do");
body("The rule is already corpus precedent, and this section only names it. In AP28, structure proposed three candidate normalisations — 1/π, 1/2π, 1/4π — and measurement eliminated two. The direction of that traffic is the entire discipline: **data may eliminate structural candidates; data may never generate them.** A number that arrives from the measurement side, however clean it looks, is a fitted parameter wearing a costume, and the corpus’s zero-fitted-parameters claim dies at the first one admitted.");
body("Applied here, the rule has teeth, because the whisper is real and it is fenced by being named. The empirically required commitment, computed backwards from the recommended central value, is 0.944 of one ε — and two clean fractions sit within reach of it: 20/21 of ε, which lands within about 60 ppm of the centre, and 17/18 of ε, which lands within a few ppm. Both arrived from the data. Neither has a forcing argument. Both come pre-dressed in a story — an exempt channel here, a refunded share there — and the stories are exactly as good as the fractions, which is to say: generated by the answer. They are recorded in this section for one purpose. Seen. Refused.");
body("And the target itself is soft, which is the final proof that digit-matching is meaningless here. Measured against the lowest laboratory determination the required fraction is 0.99 of ε; against the highest it is 0.92; the admissible window is the whole range between, four per cent wide against a formal uncertainty of 22 ppm. Any fraction in the window “fits”. Structure alone can choose, and structure already chose before the window was drawn: the axiom writes one element, once. This paper states 1×ε, states nothing finer, and arms KS-CCC.1 against every future refinement — including the author’s — that cannot show its forcing argument was written before its number.");

// ============================== 11 ==============================
h1("11 — Candidate Three");
body("Ø Predictions Part II holds the 0.69% open between two candidates and declines to pick: Candidate 1, apparatus leakage — the structural value is the unleaky value and every real measurement reads low; Candidate 2, refinement of the puncture normalisation under the KS-R.9 sub-debt. This paper enters as Candidate 3: the gap is neither in the apparatus nor in the puncture but in an unbooked provision — the structural value is the provisioned coupling, and what laboratories measure is the realised one.");
body("Candidate 3 does not merely join the list; it sharpens it, because Candidate 1 standing alone carries a strain its own published numbers expose. The gap is about 6,900 ppm. The spread between independent modern experiments is about 500 ppm. For apparatus leakage to carry the whole gap, torsion balances, beam balances and atom interferometers — radically different boundaries — would have to leak identically to within seven per cent of the effect. An apparatus effect that is over ninety per cent apparatus-independent is not an apparatus effect. It is universal, which is to say structural. Candidate 1, taken seriously, already implies a commitment-shaped universal term and a small genuinely-apparatus remainder — which is Candidate 3’s exact anatomy: a universal 1/(1 + ε), and a residual at scatter scale.");
body("The discriminator already exists in the corpus. Appendix C of Ø Predictions proposes the meta-analysis: characterise each published G experiment by its boundary geometry and test the leakage correlation. Under Candidate 1, the boundary-geometry corrections must come out near 6,900 ppm and correlate. Under Candidate 3, they must come out at scatter scale — hundreds of ppm at most. Same proposed experiment, opposite predictions, and KS-CCC.4 binds this paper to the outcome. Candidate 3 adds a falsification surface, not an escape hatch — which is the only way a third candidate earns entry into a register that was honest with two.");
body("On Candidate 2, this paper is indifferent at entry and constraining at exit. The commitment cannot rescue a wrong normalisation: carrying 1/2π to the measured value would require a commitment near eighteen ε, and 1/4π near twenty-nine — both structurally absurd. With the commitment live, 1/π remains uniquely selected among the enumerated candidates, exactly as before. The KS-R.9 sub-debt is untouched, and KS-CCC.6 states what happens if its resolution collides with §7.");

