body("*Figure 1. The seventeen determinations against the three reference values. The AP28 structural value, 6.72060 × 10⁻¹¹, sits +0.69% above the frame — outside every experiment ever performed. The realised value of The Snap v0.2 (dashed) sits at the low edge of the landscape; the CODATA 2022 band at its centre. NIST-26 (square) is the BIPM apparatus replicated at a new site by a new team: −254 ppm.*", { size: 18, after: 200 });

// ---------- 3 ----------
h1("3 — Method, fixed before computation");
body("Three commitments were fixed before any statistic was computed. First: the classification is the literature’s own method taxonomy (time-of-swing, angular-acceleration feedback, servo/strip, Fabry–Pérot suspension, beam balance, atom interferometry) — nothing invented for this analysis. Second: the calibration experiments are the three the record naturally contains — same laboratory with two methods (the HUST-18 pair), same apparatus across thirteen years (BIPM-01 against BIPM-14), and same apparatus at a new site with a new team (BIPM against NIST-26) — plus the full field range as the outer bound. Third: no retroactive boundary scoring (§0). The Candidate 1 quantity computed is the implied leakage η_i = 1 − G_i/G_struct with G_struct = 6.72060 × 10⁻¹¹; the Candidate 3 quantity is the residual r_i = G_i/G_real − 1 with G_real = 6.67192 × 10⁻¹¹.");

// ---------- 4 ----------
h1("4 — Results");
h2("4.1 — The field");
body("The sixteen CODATA inputs span 551 ppm (unweighted scatter 163 ppm about the mean), with a Birge ratio of 3.6 — consistent with CODATA’s own remedy of expanding all sixteen uncertainties by a factor of 3.9 to force consistency. The three largest normalised residuals sit on both sides of the mean: BIPM-14 (+, 7.7), JILA-18 (−, 6.8), BIPM-01 (+, 4.8). The inconsistency of G metrology is real, two-sided, and of order a few hundred ppm.");
h2("4.2 — Candidate 1’s implied leakages");
body("Computed against the structural value, the seventeen implied leakages have mean 6,967 ppm and standard deviation 157 ppm — a coefficient of variation of 2.2%. Grouped by method class, every class mean lies between 6,786 and 7,245 ppm: the entire design-to-design span is 460 ppm against a common component of 6,967 ppm. Under Candidate 1, then, at least 93% of a “boundary-geometry-dependent” effect is identical across a sealed cryogenic torsion fibre, a beam balance weighing in air-adjacent chambers, and a cloud of cold atoms in free fall with no material boundary at all. Within the scatter itself there is genuine method structure — 72% of the (small) variance lies between classes, with the strip-servo family reading high and the time-of-swing family reading low-centred — which is exactly what apparatus-dependent systematics look like at their honest size: order 10² ppm, not order 10⁴.");
h2("4.3 — The three calibrations");
body("Same laboratory, two methods (HUST-18): **45 ppm.** Same apparatus, thirteen years apart (BIPM-01 vs BIPM-14): **7.5 ppm.** Same apparatus, new site, new team (BIPM mean vs NIST-26): **254 ppm.** Full field range: **551 ppm** (386 ppm excluding the BIPM pair). Every direct calibration of apparatus-plus-site-plus-team effects lands between 7.5 and 254 ppm; the outer bound from the whole field is 551. The scale Candidate 1 requires is 6,967 — a strain factor of **28** against the strongest calibration and **13** against the outermost bound. One further published check closes a side door: Speake et al. (2023) experimentally investigated whether stray AC magnetic fields explain the BIPM outliers, and concluded they do not.");
h2("4.4 — NIST-26, the decisive point");
body("NIST-26 is the first replication in the history of high-precision G metrology: the identical BIPM torsion balance, the historically highest-reading and most divergent apparatus, rebuilt and re-run at NIST over a decade by an independent team. It returned 6.67387(38) — **254 ppm below** the BIPM determinations, landing in the centre-low region of the field, essentially on the MSL-03 value. Two consequences. The high wing of the distribution loses its independent support: the same instrument no longer reproduces it, so the BIPM-01/14 elevation is now most plausibly a site- or configuration-level systematic of order 250 ppm — measured, not conjectured. And the ceiling on demonstrated apparatus effects is now set by the cleanest possible experiment at 254 ppm, which is the number the strain factor of §4.3 stands on.");
h2("4.5 — Candidate 3’s residuals");
body("Against the realised value, the seventeen residuals have mean **+279 ppm** (SD 158). NIST-26 sits +293 ppm above the realised value and 64 ppm below the CODATA centre. Two honest readings of that mean. It is the same open item The Snap v0.2 §9 already reports — the unattributed −0.036% — now seen from the data side; the meta-analysis re-derives the paper’s own held-open residual rather than discovering a new one, which is consistency, not confirmation. And its sign is not leakage-shaped: residual downward leakage would deepen it, not explain it. Published upward mechanisms of exactly this order exist — fibre anelasticity biases time-of-swing determinations upward (the Kuroda hypothesis, of order 1/πQ, ~10²ppm; LANL-97 was designed as its test) — but this paper does not invoke them as the explanation. The residual stays open, and KS-CCC.3 watches the direction: the one post-2018 replication-grade determination moved the field’s newest point *downward*, toward the realised value.");

// ---------- 5 ----------
h1("5 — Verdict at Tier 1");
body("**Candidate 1 — strained to near-exclusion.** For apparatus leakage to carry the 0.69%, an effect AP06 defines as boundary-geometry-dependent must be ≥93% independent of boundary geometry; free-falling atoms with no absorbing boundary must leak within a few per cent of a sealed cryogenic fibre; and the same-apparatus replication that bounds site-and-team effects at 254 ppm must be wrong by a factor of 28. The remaining escape is a leakage mechanism common to all boundaries — which is by definition not apparatus leakage but a universal term, i.e., Candidate 3’s anatomy under Candidate 1’s name. Tier 1 cannot formally close Candidate 1 (Tier 2’s blind coding could in principle reveal a hidden common geometric factor), but the burden has moved decisively.");
body("**Candidate 3 — consistent, and not confirmed.** The tier’s discriminating observable comes out exactly where Candidate 3 requires it: apparatus effects demonstrated at 7.5–460 ppm, with genuine method structure inside the scatter for residual leakage to explain at its honest size. What the tier cannot do is confirm the magnitude — the admissible window of §10 of The Snap forbids that claim from data this soft — and the +279 ppm mean residual above the realised value stands open, in the same ledger line v0.2 already carries. Consistency at the discriminator, plus one open residual, plus a favourable direction from the newest point: that is the full honest statement, and nothing stronger will be claimed from it.");
body("**KS-CCC.4 — partially executed, survives, sharpened.** The switch’s Tier 1 condition — that demonstrated apparatus corrections be of scatter scale, not gap scale — is met. Full execution is Tier 2.");

// ---------- 6 ----------
h1("6 — Limits");
body("Seventeen points, several correlated; statistics descriptive throughout; one value (UCI-14) confirmed through the CODATA table and its published relative uncertainty rather than a second primary source; method classes are the literature’s but coarse — BIPM-01 combined two extraction methods on one instrument and is classed by its instrument; and the strain argument bounds apparatus effects by their demonstrated scale, which is an empirical ceiling, not a theorem. None of these limits touches the order-of-magnitude structure of the result: no reading of this dataset produces a 6,900 ppm apparatus-dependent effect with a 2% design variation.");

// ---------- 7 ----------
h1("7 — Tier 2, pre-registered");
body("Tier 2 is the full Appendix C: per-apparatus boundary coding, done blind. Protocol, fixed now, before any coding: a coder who does not know which apparatus produced which value receives the seventeen apparatus descriptions with all values and identifying results redacted, and codes five dimensions — (1) test-mass containment: free fall / suspended in sealed chamber; (2) source-mass position: inside or outside the vacuum boundary; (3) enclosure closure: number and material of boundary layers between source and test mass; (4) suspension element: none / fibre / strip, with published Q where available (the anelasticity proxy); (5) boundary sphericity class per AP06 S5. Values are unblinded only after coding is complete, and the correlations are computed once. Success criteria, stated in advance: Candidate 1 requires the coded leakage proxies to support a common apparatus term of order 6,000 ppm or more and to correlate with the deviations from the structural value; Candidate 3 requires the proxies to correlate with the ≤500 ppm scatter (in particular, Q with the time-of-swing elevations) and to support no gap-scale common term. The coding task is sized for the independent verifier, after the canon sync, under the sign-off protocol. One further watch item costs nothing: the next CODATA adjustment will incorporate NIST-26, and the direction its recommended value moves is exactly what KS-CCC.3 watches.");

// ---------- 8 ----------
h1("8 — One page for the author");
body("The corpus proposed an experiment before it knew which answer it needed. Tier 1 of that experiment is now run, on seventeen published determinations including one that did not exist when the proposal was written. The result: the effect Candidate 1 requires would have to be twenty-eight times larger than the largest apparatus effect ever demonstrated — including by the first true replication in the field’s history — and ninety-three per cent identical across boundaries as different as a free-falling atom cloud and a sealed cryogenic fibre. The effect Candidate 3 requires is exactly the size the record shows. Nothing here confirms the commitment’s magnitude, and the +279 ppm residual above the realised value stays open where v0.2 put it. But the division of labour — a universal term for the gap, honest leakage for the scatter — is now the only reading of the published record that does not ask an apparatus effect to stop being one. The blade for the remaining step is written above, and it is in someone else’s hands, which is where blades belong.");

